---
.title = "Game Jams of Fall 2026",
.date = .date("2026-11-19T00:00:00"),
.layout = "post.shtml",
---

Hey guys.

I've been wanting to talk about game jams for quite a bit, but I felt like
it'd be better to wait till I'm actually done with all of them this semester.

Making game is very fun, but also very, very tiring. Creating a game in 2 days
can be very rewarding, but they also burn me out quite a bit. That being said, I
actually participated in 3 different jams this semester, thanks in part to the UA
Game Dev Club (of which I am an officer).

Overall, I am very proud of all the games I created. This was my first time
participating in weekend long jams as opposed to week long jams, and it was odd.
Weirdly enough, I feel like I enjoyed the weekend long jams more. The smaller timeframe
forced me to focus my ideas and scope, and as a result the games felt more comeplete
by the end. With the week long jams, I often overshoot with my plans, and anytime
that I am busy during the week, I feel like I could have used that time to make my
game better.

Anyways, the games were as followed (and are also available [here](/games))
 - [September 2026: Moving Up](https://plasmaoj15.itch.io/moving-up)
    
    A rage game about climbing up a mountain by possessing enemies.

    Created for Texas Game Jam 2026, Theme: Chain Reaction

    This game was really fun to make. Unlike most jams I've done, this concept was
    the first one I came up with. I started with the idea of chaining enemies into
    each other, and just added the idea of moving upwards to it.
    
    Fun fact: I finished the main mechanic of possession and chaining within the
    first 6 hours of the jam, the rest of the time was dedicated to creating the
    actual level of the mountain, as well as the gimmicks.

 - [October 2026: Blue Lou's TV Repair](https://plasmaoj15.itch.io/blue-lous-tv-repair)

    A game about fixing old analogue televisions.

    Created for Ludum Dare 60, Theme: Old Tech

    This game was made in a group, which outside of [TimeSpace](https://plasmaoj15.itch.io/timespace),
    is the only game made for a jam that I have done in a group. One team member even
    worked with me on TimeSpace, so it was fun to work together again. This game, while
    not entirely different from concept, does have a few changes. My original idea was
    the repairing of tech on a spaceship being attacked by aliens. You would have to work
    on the tech, then turn around and blast the aliens if they got too close. This idea
    would become merged with my teamates idea of creating various microgames inspired by
    old tech. I really enjoyed creating the art for this game, especially the loss/win
    cutscenes, they are probably my favorite part of the game.
    
    Fun Fact: The name "Blue Lou" is heavily inspired by the game [Green Neil's Nuclear Extravaganza](https://spino9999.itch.io/green-neils-nuclear-extravaganza),
    a competator in the Texas Game Jam 2026. The game became a meme within the UA Game Dev club for
    its "green mode." Blue Lou even got its own green mode in the settings!

 - [November 2026: Burn Up, Burn Out](https://plasmaoj15.itch.io/burn-up-burn-out-jam)

    A puzzle platformer where you play as a little flame dude, who is constantly burning out!

    Created for the UA Fall 2026 Game Jam, Theme: Burn Out

    This game was made with two goals in mind: Animate a little guy for the protagonist,
    and use the Godot lighting and particle system. Everything else was secondary. Luckily, I
    really liked the result, even if I never got to create sound or spend as much time on it.
    This games concept did not change much throghout the concept stages, however it was more
    focused on puzzles rather than speed originally. Instead of prioritizing not burning out,
    the game prioritized puzzle solving and quick thinking. I still think this is true of the
    first non-tutorial level, but everything after that focuses on speed and skill as opposed to puzzles.
    I do plan to continue work on this game, I feel like I could make a pretty interesting game
    with some more time and tuning. Plus, it'll be nice to have a long term project aside from
    this website to keep coming back too.

    Fun Fact: The title of this game is a reference to [Porter Robinson's 2021 song, Musician](https://youtu.be/q-74HTjRbuY),
    which is one of my favorite songs of his.
